Pasting text from other programs (round 19): blocks of code become cells, line breaks and tabs are made the
notebook's own, and copied cells come back as the same cells.

=== code pasted at an insertion point makes one Input cell per block between blank lines
--- before
‖
--- keys
Clip("f(x_) := x^2\n\nf(3)\nf(4)\n\n\n") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% cell
f(x_) := x^2

#% cell
f(3)
f(4)▮

=== blank lines inside brackets keep a block together
--- before
‖
--- keys
Clip("g(x_) := {\n    a = x\n\n    a + 1\n}\n\ng(2)") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% cell
g(x_) := {
    a = x

    a + 1
}

#% cell
g(2)▮

=== other programs' line breaks and leading tabs become the notebook's
--- before
#% cell
▮
--- keys
Clip("h(x_) := {\r\n\tx\r\n}") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% cell
h(x_) := {
    x
}▮

=== prose pasted at an insertion point of a Text style stays one cell
--- before
‖
--- keys
Alt+7 Clip("one\n\ntwo") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% text
one

two▮

=== cells copied as text and pasted back are the same cells
--- before
#% cell ◆
a = 1

#% cell ◆
b = a + 1
--- keys
Ctrl+C Ctrl+End Clip("a = 1\n\nb = a + 1") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% cell
a = 1

#% cell
b = a + 1

#% cell
a = 1

#% cell
b = a + 1▮

=== pasted blocks are one undo step
--- before
#% cell
x
‖
--- keys
Clip("p\n\nq") Ctrl+V Ctrl+Z
--- after
#% cell
x
‖

=== a call pasted at an insertion point keeps its closing bracket
--- before
‖
--- keys
Clip("w = x^2/sqrt(y) + surd(a, 3)") Ctrl+V
--- after
#% cell
w = x^2/sqrt(y) + surd(a, 3)▮
